// GENERATED by `ggen sync` from ontology/bcinr.ttl + imports -- DO NOT EDIT.
//
// One test per refusal variant the graph claims is reachable. Each runs the
// witness recorded against that variant and asserts the refusal it actually
// produces.
//
// Why this file and not a comment: `bc:falsifier` used to be prose. Prose
// cannot fail, so when the soundness gate started catching the
// `IrreducibleFragment` witness first, the recorded falsifier silently stopped
// being true and nothing noticed. A generated test is the same claim in a
// medium where it can be wrong out loud.
//
// A variant with no witness does not appear here. It is not thereby exempt:
// `ontology/shapes.ttl` refuses to admit a refusal case that carries neither a
// witness nor a written argument for why none exists, so absence from this
// file is always accompanied by a recorded reason rather than by silence.
//
// `#[allow(unused_imports)]` below is load-bearing, not sloppiness.
// `bc:rustFalsifierPrelude` is declared once per *taxonomy* and replayed into
// every case of it, so a case that needs only part of the shared prelude is the
// expected shape rather than a defect. Making the prelude per-case instead
// would trade one honest allow for N near-duplicate graph facts that drift
// against each other -- the same duplication this whole projection exists to
// remove. Under `-D warnings` the allow is what keeps that choice compilable.
{% for c in results %}
/// `{{ c.taxonomy_label }}::{{ c.case_name }}` -- {{ c.case_doc }}
#[test]
#[allow(unused_imports)]
fn {{ c.fn_name }}() {
    {{ c.prelude }}
    {{ c.setup }}
    let observed = {{ c.expr }};
    assert!(
        matches!(observed, {{ c.pattern }}),
        "the graph declares {{ c.taxonomy_label }}::{{ c.case_name }} reachable by this witness, but it produced {:?}",
        observed
    );
}
{% endfor %}
